{%- comment -%}
  HIVOLT — templates/page.size-guide.liquid
  ─────────────────────────────────────────
  Alternate page template. In the admin: Pages → Size Guide → Template suffix →
  "size-guide". Renders inside layout/theme.liquid, which supplies <main>.

  Styles live in site/theme/pages.css. build-theme.py concatenates that into
  the single assets/hivolt.css that layout/theme.liquid already links, after
  theme-hivolt.css — so there is no second stylesheet to add.

  This page deliberately carries NO size table.

  Every product's own description already contains the supplier's measured
  table for that exact style, in inches and centimetres, for the sizes that
  style is actually cut in. A second table here would be a copy — and a copy
  of a measurement is the thing that goes stale and starts contradicting the
  garment. So this page explains how to use the tables and where they are,
  and the numbers stay in one place.

  The measuring instructions below are generic tailoring method, not a claim
  about any HIVOLT garment. Nothing here asserts a fit, a stretch allowance or
  a "runs small" — none of that is in the supplier data, so none of it is said.
{%- endcomment -%}

<nav class="crumb wrap" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
  <ol>
    <li><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}">Home</a></li>
    <li aria-current="page">{{ page.title | default: 'Size guide' | escape }}</li>
  </ol>
</nav>

<section class="hero hero--doc wrap">
  <p class="mono hero__eyebrow">Sizing</p>
  <h1 class="hero__h">The table<br>is on the<br><em>product.</em></h1>
  <p class="hero__sub">
    Every style publishes the supplier's own measured table — inches and
    centimetres, for the sizes that style is actually cut in. This page is how
    to read it.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="pg wrap">
  <div class="pg__cols">

    <div class="rte">
      {%- if page.content != blank -%}
        {{ page.content }}
      {%- else -%}
        <p>
          Sizing runs differ by category, because different garments are cut to
          different blocks. Open any product and the measured table for that
          style sits on the page.
        </p>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>

    <aside class="pg__aside">
      <p class="mono pg__aside-h">How to measure</p>
      <div class="rte rte--tight">
        <p>
          Measure over the underwear you would train in, with the tape flat and
          level — snug, not pulled tight. Compare against the garment table on
          the product page.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Chest / bust</strong> — around the fullest part, tape level under the arms.</li>
          <li><strong>Waist</strong> — around the narrowest part, usually just above the navel.</li>
          <li><strong>Hip</strong> — around the fullest part, feet together.</li>
          <li><strong>Inseam</strong> — from the crotch seam to where you want the hem to sit.</li>
          <li><strong>Length</strong> — from the high point of the shoulder straight down.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="mono pg__aside-h pg__aside-h--gap">Reading the table</p>
      <div class="rte rte--tight">
        <p>
          Product tables are <strong>garment measurements laid flat</strong>, not
          body measurements. A 20&Prime; chest figure is the garment across the
          chest — roughly a 40&Prime; body measurement once it goes around.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both units are printed. The centimetre column is the supplier's
          original; the inch column is its conversion.
        </p>
      </div>
    </aside>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="pub">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec__head">
      <h2 class="sec__h">Where the numbers come from</h2>
      <span class="mono">Supplier specification</span>
    </div>

    <div class="pub__grid">
      <div class="pub__c">
        <p class="pub__n">01</p>
        <h3 class="pub__t">One table per style</h3>
        <p class="pub__d">
          Each product carries the table for that garment only — the sizes it is
          cut in, and no others. Nothing is interpolated to fill a gap.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pub__c">
        <p class="pub__n">02</p>
        <h3 class="pub__t">Metric first</h3>
        <p class="pub__d">
          The supplier measures in centimetres. The inch figures beside them are
          conversions of those measurements, not a separate set.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pub__c">
        <p class="pub__n">03</p>
        <h3 class="pub__t">Weight is published too</h3>
        <p class="pub__d">
          Alongside the table, each product states its fibre composition and
          fabric weight in g/m&sup2;. How heavy a knit is changes how it sits as
          much as the measurements do.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="pg wrap">
  <div class="sec__head">
    <h2 class="sec__h">Find a table</h2>
    <span class="mono">Open any product</span>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Link only to collections that exist and hold something. A dead category
    link on the page a buyer opens before adding to cart is worse than a
    shorter list.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign cats = 'womens-activewear,mens-activewear,leggings,sports-bras,shorts,outerwear-hoodies' | split: ',' -%}
  <div class="sheet">
    {%- for h in cats -%}
      {%- assign c = collections[h] -%}
      {%- if c != blank and c.products_count > 0 -%}
        <div class="sheet__row">
          <span class="sheet__k"><a href="{{ c.url }}">{{ c.title | escape }}</a></span>
          <span class="sheet__v">{{ c.products_count }} styles</span>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
    <div class="sheet__row">
      <span class="sheet__k"><a href="{{ routes.all_products_collection_url }}">Everything</a></span>
      <span class="sheet__v">Full catalogue</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="pg__qw wrap">
  <div class="pg__q">
    <p class="pg__q-t">
      <span class="mono">Still between two sizes?</span>
      Send the measurements you took and the style you're looking at. We'll read
      them against the same table you're looking at — we don't have a different
      one.
    </p>
    {%- assign contact_page = pages['contact-us'] | default: pages['contact'] -%}
    <a class="btn" href="{{ contact_page.url | default: '/pages/contact-us' }}">Ask about fit</a>
  </div>
</section>
